pkg/locspec, pkg/proc: files by import path, closures in closures

A FILE:LINE location matches a suffix of a source file's path on disk,
so a file of a package built from a directory whose path does not end
in the package's import path, such as a module that go.mod replaces
with a local directory, could not be named by its import path:

	trace x github.com/acme/widgets/widgets.go:218

said "not found" when go.mod had

	replace github.com/acme/widgets => ../widgets

and only a suffix of the path on disk worked, so command files differed
between checkouts. BinaryInfo.PackageFileAliases lists each file of a Go
package under the package's import path (from its compile unit, in the
directory of the package's functions), and NormalLocationSpec.Find also
matches those names. A file of an external test package, pkg_test, is
listed under pkg too.

A function location splits at dots into at most three parts, package,
receiver and name, so the name of a closure in a closure, as stacks
print it, did not parse:

	trace x example.com/m/harness.TestReadYourWrites.func1.1

said "location not found", as did pkg.F.func1.1 and F.func1.1 without
the package. FuncLocationSpec.Suffix now holds such a name, and a
function matches it when its full name is the name or ends with it
after a '/' or a '.'.

Both are generic fixes for upstream Delve.

